After a week or so of neglecting my studies, I've finally managed to 
ascend in Nethack for the first time ever!

Actually, it was a bit of a letdown. I tried my hardest to earn YASD in 
a few places, but the RNG was just too kind to me this game. I used a 
whopping SEVENTEEN wishes over the course of the game, and I still had 
two charges left on a wand of wishing when I ascended.

I've been playing Archeologists almost exclusively for years now. Most 
people here say that Valkyrie is the easiest, but when I tried them, I 
never got very far. The main reason I'm so fond of the Archeologist is 
because of the pickaxe. That thing is just SO AMAZING! It's a weapon. It 
finds secret doors for me. It opens locked doors without injuring my 
legs. It opens chests. It gets me money from magic memory vaults. I also 
like being able to identify any gem that I find, and starting with three 
food rations means that you don't have to worry about food early on. The 
bullwhip is not as useless as some guides have claimed; I use it to make 
monsters drop their weapons. A dwarf with a broad pick is a lot more 
dangerous than one with bare hands. It's just an all-around excellent 
set of starting equipment, except for that leather jacket and fedora.

Here's how my game went. I found an ebony wand on dungeon level 5 or so, 
and engraved with it. Wow - a wand of wishing! What to wish for... ah, 
yes, Next, a blessed scroll of charging. (Should have been 2.) By the 
time I had exhaused my wand, I had a blessed magic marker, a blessed 
fixed greased +2 robe, blessed +2 grey dragon scale mail, blessed fixed 
+2 gauntlets of power, and a blessed bag of holding.

I found lots more goodies in the early and middle levels of the dungeon. 
An amulet I picked up early on turned out to be an amulet of life 
saving. I found lots of spellbooks, including one of magic mapping, 
detect treasure, and detect monsters. I was armored to the teeth with 
good drops from monsters (a blessed +2 orcish helm, +0 iron shoes, +1 
large shield) and the spoils of the wand. Nothing could touch me!

I always go to Sokoban before the Gnomish mines; I find that I tend to 
die in the Mines if I go there first. Along the way, my pet housecat 
named Mischief ate a chameleon corpse and turned into a pet DWARF KING. 
That was pretty nice, I had a reasonably powerful pet that could outfit 
itself with all the goodies that I'd been coming across. I had also 
found a magic whistle before that. Sokoban had a bag of holding instead 
of an amluet of reflection. I took it with me anyway for a while until I 
eventually discarded it.

Sacrificing at an altar in the mines gave me Ogresmasher and Cleaver, 
but I kept it up until I got the weapon I wanted - the blessed rustproof 
Vorpal Blade, which I enchanted to +6. (I had not yet identified the 
scroll of enchant armor, but I had enchant weapon.) I had some unholy 
water on me, so I used it on some scrolls of create monster to get 
plenty of sacrifice food. I was now an unstoppable killing machine. 
Nothing could stop me from getting that amulet!

After Sokoban but before entering the Gnomish Mines, I realized that 
perhaps I ought to take advantage of this wand of polymorph that I'd 
been carrying. I zapped my pet dwarf king with it, and he turned into a 
BALROG. Neat! He moved slowly and didn't really like hanging around me, 
but with the magic whistle I could make him kill stuff for me. A potion 
had made be permanently invisible (after testing it with a blessed 
unicorn horn), so I couldn't get into shops (yeah, I know about mummy 
wrappings) but my pet would happily kill shopkeepers for me. I took him 
through the Mines and down to Minetown. Forgive me, but I had my pet 
kill the lighting store owner, too. :( He also killed the coaligned 
priest so I couldn't buy any protection with all that gold and gems I'd 
been carrying around.

I went further down into the mines, and found a luckstone before I 
reached the final level. I blessed it and went to the Gnome King's wine 
cellar anyway for a backup in case something bad happened to my first 
one. I climbed back out of the mines and proceeded deeper into the 
Dungeons of Doom. Among the goodies I found were a coaligned temple (I 
made sure that my pet wouldn't kill the priest), a ring of conflict, a 
Hawaiian shirt, and +0 water walking boots.

I found a polymorph trap on the level with the temple, and I started 
thinking. (Here's the first place where I almost had YASD.) I could 
polymorph into a xorn with my ring of polymorph control, eat my ring of 
fire resistance and of teleport control for the intrinsics, and then use 
my wand of polymorph to change back. I took off all my armor, stepped on 
the trap, and turned into a xorn. I ate my rings, but I didn't get any 
intrinsics. The ring of fire resistance was diamond and therefor 
inedible. I did NOT eat the ring of conflict, but I was a bloody idiot 
and ATE MY RING OF POLYMORPH CONTROL! To make matters worse, my wand of 
polymorph was out of charges! I keep zapping myself with the empty wand, 
hoping for one last charge. I eventually get one and turn into... a 
hobgoblin. With THREE STINKING HIT POINTS!!!

Okay, now, how do I reverse the situation? I put on what armor I could 
and went to the temple. I prayed on the altar, hoping for some divine 
intervention. I "felt much better" and had six hit points instead of 
three. Thanks for nothing, Camaxil. I wondered if my amulet of life 
saving would turn me back into a human, so I seriously considered dying 
on purpose. However, I didn't actually get to test this theory out. I 
just smacked a few monsters around with my Vorpal Blade and my -15 AC, 
and to my great surprise, I returned to human form! I put on the rest of 
my armor and picked my equipment back up.

I reached the quest level. On the floor outside the College was ANOTHER 
WAND OF WISHING!!! First things first - another blessed magic marker so 
I could write scrolls of charging. Next, a blessed spellbook of identify 
(so I didn't have to use up the marker on identify scrolls again) and 
some blessed fixed +2 boots of speed. I didn't have enough EXP to do the 
quest, so I moved on.

Somewhere along the way, I sat on a throne and was asked to genocide a 
species. I chose "master mind flayer" although I really should have 
chosen "disenchanter" because I ended up using up a blessed scroll of 
genocide at the castle to get rid of them. Everything I had was either 
rustproof or covered, so it didn't matter at all. I also genocided all 
liches once I reached the castle. It would have been better to genocide 
all species of mind flayers, because one of them sucked my brains on the 
last floor of Gehennom. Oddly enough, I never had a problem with ; at 
all. Maybe the ring of free action I picked up had something to do with it?

When I got to Medusa's level, I put on my water walking boots and 
started killing stuff. I put on a towel and went after Medusa, but she 
had a wand of digging and ran away. I got a cursed shield of reflection 
from Perseus's statue, which I promptly blessed. At this point, I 
stopped to do what I should have done a while ago; enchant all my armor. 
I get everything up to +5, for a grand total of -51 AC! That's insane!

I chased after Medusa through some maze levels, and she turned my balrog 
into a statue. I did kill her, though, and went back up to do the quest. 
  The Quest Nemesis ran away from me, but I eventually took him out and 
returned with the Orb of Detection and the Bell of Opening. I tamed a 
large cat that I named Mischief II, but I killed it when I displaced it 
into the water. I did some sacrificing and Camaxil was mollified, so I 
just continued on by myself. With AC -51, a +6 Vorpal Blade, and all the 
other stuff I had, what could possibly be a serious threat? As it turns 
out, not very much!

I reached the castle and saw that there were liches and disenchanters 
inside, so I used the two blessed scrolls of genocide that I had been 
saving. I had lost my ring of conflict to lightning somewhere along the 
way, so I wished for a replacement - "blessed fixed ring of conflict." 
Bad idea; I got a cursed fixed -2 ring of increase accuracy. I threw it 
into the moat. I then wished again for a "blessed ring of conflict" and 
got it. I played my bugle to lower the drawbridge and watched the chaos 
ensue. I ended up with yet another wand of wishing, and I STILL hadn't 
used up the one from the quest home yet! I didn't have a ring of 
levitation to go over the trap doors, so I wished for one. I killed and 
ate several dragons along the way so I picked up a lot of resistances, 
including disentegration resistance.

It was time to face the Valley of the Dead and Gehennom. The valley was 
uneventful; I didn't get any wraith corpses. This was only the second 
time I'd ever made it this far. Last time, I died on Orcus's level when 
he summoned too many monsters form me to deal with, one of which was 
Demogorgon. I didn't want that to happen again, so I bribed two demon 
princes, took Jubilex apart from the inside, and chopped up Orcus before 
he could teleport away to the stairs leading up, grabbing a wand of 
death with four charges in it. I actually entered Orcus's lair from 
below thanks to some annoying holes that I had a tendency to fall 
through. The maze levels themselves were pretty easy; I just used magic 
mapping and my pickaxe to get through quickly. A smoky potion (of extra 
healing) granted me a wish; I didn't know what else to get, so I asked 
for a "blessed figurine of an Archon." I put it in my bag and forgot 
about it. Vlad died easily when I reached his tower.

The vibrating square level was annoying. For the heck of it, I decided 
to pickaxe every wall until I found the vibrating square. Also, I found 
a succubus; because potions of gain ability had raised every stat to 18 
except Charisma (which was 12) I decided to use her to get to level 30, 
which I did. I had an unfortunate encounter with a regular mind flayer, 
who sucked my brains while I was fooling around with the succubus. Oh 
well; my unicorn horn got my INT back to 18, but forgetting stuff is 
annoying. At least I could still use magic mapping and identify. Time to 
move on. I used my figurine and got myself a pet Sunsword-wielding Archon.

It was time to take on the Wizard of Yendor. Orcus's wand of death took 
him out. I took all the invocation artifacts to the vibrating square, 
performed the invocation, and raided Moloch's sanctum for the Amulet of 
Yendor.

The climb back up was annoying. Unlike trolls, tinning the Wizard of 
Yendor didn't keep him from coming back. One time I tried to fight 
Rodney with Vorpal Blade instead of just zapping him; he started to fill 
the level with monsters and my Archon died. I eventually just zapped 
him. I had to wish for another magic marker to write charging scrolls 
for the wand of death. I kept climbing for a while and then realized 
that my Amulet of Yendor was missing. I went back down for it and 
eventually found it, then climbed up. Darn that mysterious force!

Well, I went up and up and up, and finally escaped the dungeon. I 
arrived on the Elemental Plane of Earth, and started to try to dig my 
way around. I had another unfortunate incident here. I found an 
unidentified potion (sky blue) and assuming it was just something I had 
forgotton about, I quaffed it.

WRONG.

It was a potion of polymorph.

I was now a gray dragon. At least I was something that was pretty 
strong, but I lost my robe and shirt in the transformation. I fought for 
  a while and killed a bunch of elves and elementals. Umber hulks chewed 
through most of the level for me. Eventually I turned human again, but 
it was difficult to find all my stuff again. I still had the castle 
wand, so I wished for a robe to replace the one I lost. I could only get 
+2 from a wish though, so my AC was down to -43. It didn't matter; I was 
still a nearly unstoppable killing machine.

Next up was the Plane of Air. I had trouble moving around until I put on 
my ring of levitation. The monsters fought each other, so I didn't have 
too much trouble with air elementals. Finding the portal was tricky, but 
I eventually saw some monster fly into it.

The Plane of Fire was annoying. All those enemies were just hitting each 
other with fire-resistant attacks and not doing any damage. I took off 
my ring of conflict to get rid of the endless messages, and started to 
try to clean up while looking for the portal.

The annoying part was that Archons were starting to spawn, and they 
would summon other monsters and stun me with their gaze attack. I 
smacked away at stuff for a while until I saw my HP start to drop by 
about 100. I put the ring of conflict back on and burned Elbereth in the 
floor. After a while, things calmed down and I beheaded a bunch of 
Archons. It took an awfully long time to find the portal, but I 
eventually ended up in the Plane of Water.

The Plane of Water was really easy. I just wandered inside my bubble 
until I found the portal, and then stepped in, forgetting to take off my 
ring of conflict.

The Astral Plane wasn't really that difficult, but it was annoying. The 
priests filled the area with monsters and Death took my maximum HP down 
to 200, but eventually I found the proper altar (it was the third one I 
checked) and offered the Amulet of Yendor. Kind of a letdown, really.

I didn't save the output of the DYWYPI stuff; my inventory didn't seem 
to want to copy/paste. Oh well.

Now that I've finally gotten that blasted Amulet, I'll be able to 
actually do some schoolwork. Wish me luck!

      - Doug


